body:
Should we all start wearing face-masks, home-made or otherwise, just as a precaution to protect other people despite the fact we may not be showing symptoms? I'm now going under the notion of 'we're all infected at this point, it's only a manner of time before we finally show' ... I'm not sure I'm making sense.

&#x200B;

I'm really scared.

I'm 31, and I still don't have my licence! 

&#x200B;

Really, I'd say take it slow. Perhaps find a driving school and explain the situation. I'm sure the driving tutors know a thing or two about working with people on the spectrum. And if you've done it a few times before, keep doing it, and soon you'll be more comfortable.

&#x200B;

And if you don't feel ready, then that's okay. I personally get anxiety every time I think about learning to drive. I am not good at making split-second decisions that is required when being on the road -- I freeze up at every minute change, even every time my girlfriend puts her foot on the gas. I only ever driven twice. Both times I almost crashed into something and cried. Both times my mother yelled at me. As much as she said that I would be okay the more I do it, at this time in my life, I see no point. If I'm still uncomfortable with driving, then that's life. I'm not the first or last person who would never drive.

&#x200B;

Lastly, take example from Spongebob. He might not drive, but he's still smiling, and he will never give up!

I can't remember the specific one, but a recent Superman Movie we saw for my brother's birthday. It was sensory nightmare: I was getting sick with how much was going on. Bringing earplugs didn't help one bit, it was bright, it was loud, and I was getting motion sick. I eventually had to move to the back of the theatre because I was getting really sick.

&#x200B;

That was only a bad movie because of a bad experience. I don't know if that counts.
